* Export a plain SQL dump
Use a pg_dump version at least as new as the source server. Replace the connection details and database names below.
@code bash
pg_dump -h localhost -U username -d my_db -f db.sql
@end
A database dump does not include cluster-wide roles; create any required roles on the target separately.

* Restore plain SQL into a new database
@code bash
createdb -h localhost -U username restored_db
psql -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -h localhost -U username -d restored_db -f db.sql
@end
The target must be empty. Stop on errors and check the restored tables before using the database.

* Export a custom-format archive
@code bash
pg_dump -h localhost -U username -d my_db -Fc -f db.dump
pg_restore --list db.dump
@end
Custom format supports selecting objects during restore; it is not a SQL script.

* Restore the archive into another new database
@code bash
createdb -h localhost -U username restored_archive_db
pg_restore --exit-on-error -h localhost -U username -d restored_archive_db db.dump
@end
Use `pg_restore` for archives and `psql` for plain SQL. A failed restore may leave partial data; investigate before retrying.

Related: {:postgres:}[PostgreSQL], {:psql:}[psql]. References: {https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgdump.html}[pg_dump], {https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgrestore.html}[pg_restore].
